						<a href="/forum/t-13894041/origami-brain">Origami Brain</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4836816">
									

<p>Got a PM about this, took a quick look.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'll be honest, I immediately am iffy on the core anomaly because it combines two extremely commonly seen formulaic approaches (&quot;thing that kills in an oddly specific way for no reason&quot; and &quot;thing that forces people to act a certain way&quot;) and the only narrative background is &quot;AWCY? is involved somehow&quot;. I'm also skeptical that an AWCY? anartist would expect someone to consistently unravel the brains of the individuals who died from the anomaly just to see some hint at an indication of affiliation.</p>
<p>At any rate, even if the message you've got in mind is something that I can see an AWCY? anartist spreading, the fact that the videos were apparently targeting <em>children</em> really doesn't sit well with me and I would downvote pretty immediately for the gratuitous violence. Given that a real-life child has died after attempting to follow a DIY-type video (Ann Reardon's video here: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CEQaYdvs478">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CEQaYdvs478</a> discusses the matter quite well, I think) the approach you've used feels somewhat tactless to me as a reader.</p>
<p>There's also the rather unnecessarily callous Foundation response:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Follows some investigation logs, a series of testings to discover details about the anomaly (how much of the video one must watch to become affected, how long the symptoms take to appear, how long it takes to die, etc)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If it's clear that exposure to the anomaly causes test subjects to die, the Foundation has no reason to continue testing something like this. The Foundation has no reason to assume that the information of how long it takes for an individual to die due to the anomaly is even close to worth a test subject's life, let alone the lives of multiple test subjects.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Overall&#8230; I'm not sure this is workable as currently presented. I myself wouldn't be interested in reading a full draft of this unless it were basically rewritten to address the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>don't make the anomaly just kill people. If you really want to write body horror, have there be something more unique than just &quot;it kills people by turning part of their body into something else&quot;</li>
<li>have the motive of the anomaly's creator be clearer and more relatable. Thus far the generic and kind of stereotypical &quot;art is dead, here is a reminder of what true art is&quot; message intended to be spread through the deaths of innocents feels both disingenuous and hypocritical. How can this person expect people to grow artistically if they're killing said people before that growth can occur? And for those who are observing the effects, wouldn't that just discourage them from ever attempting origami on their own?</li>
<li>address the fact that sometimes people just watch the videos for inspiration, or because they're bored, or because they want to try something out. Not everyone watches content farm material because they think the channel needs support or because they feel like a patron of the arts. People who genuinely don't know about why content farms are bad shouldn't be punished for happening to watch a video posted by a content farm.</li>
</ul>

							</div>

								
					
